WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.420
Hi, I'm Sandra, from HEARa.

2
00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.620
Welcome to Day 4 of our 10 
day Lipreading Challenge, 

3
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.120
designed to help you develop and 
practice your lip reading skills! 

4
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.900
Hearing loss can have a 
profound impact on your life. 

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.060
It touches every interaction. 

6
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.498
It can affect your career, your relationships,

7
00:00:21.498 --> 00:00:22.619
and your happiness.

8
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.900
Join me to explore tips and 
tricks on this YouTube channel 

9
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.480
to bring confidence and clarity to relationships
in all areas of your life. 

10
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.840
Before we get started with our 
lip reading practice today, 

11
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.920
I wanted to tell you about a way
to teach the people in your life 

12
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.780
to speak more clearly
so that you can read their lips more easily. 

13
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.820
A client once said to me, 
if everyone talked like you, 

14
00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.584
I wouldn't have any problems with my hearing!

15
00:00:53.584 --> 00:00:58.239
Hearing care professionals do tend to speak very clearly.

16
00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:00.949
It's one of the occupational hazards.

17
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.455
We also have battery stickers 
anywhere and everywhere,

18
00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:08.168
and we talk fairly loud to everyone.

19
00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:10.435
I don't remember being taught

20
00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:13.462
how to talk to people with hearing loss

21
00:01:13.462 --> 00:01:15.461
in any of my Audiology courses.

22
00:01:15.461 --> 00:01:20.713
It's something that we all 
learned from experience.

23
00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.510
In other words, people are teachable.

24
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.000
I would caution against asking 
people to speak clearly, 

25
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.480
most people have the perception

26
00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.520
that they're already speaking clearly.

27
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.560
If they feel defensive, 

28
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.686
they may not hear your message.

29
00:01:39.686 --> 00:01:44.880
If there's a person in your life who talks way too fast or mumbles 

30
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.439
or becomes practically inaudible 
at the end of a sentence, 

31
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.160
there's a way that you can 
teach them to do otherwise. 

32
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.073
It's called the chameleon effect.

33
00:01:58.073 --> 00:02:00.431
The chameleon effect is at play

34
00:02:00.576 --> 00:02:01.852
when you lean forward

35
00:02:01.852 --> 00:02:05.460
because the other person leans forward.

36
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.580
We unconsciously and unintentionally
mimic people that we are interacting with. 

37
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.640
We are also chameleons when 
it comes to rate of speech, 

38
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.480
accents, and just about any form of behaviour. 

39
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:29.280
I suggest you say the sentences
from today's practice in the mirror silently. 

40
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.958
Look at yourself as you're saying them.

41
00:02:32.958 --> 00:02:34.750
How clearly do YOU speak?

42
00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.920
The clarity of YOUR speech is what it's going to make the chameleon effect 

43
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.760
work for you.

44
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.862
If someone's talking too fast, slow down your own speech.

45
00:02:47.862 --> 00:02:50.100
Crisp up the consonants.

46
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.902
Pause between sentences.

47
00:02:53.902 --> 00:02:55.560
Say each word.

48
00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.040
Don't let your words run together. 

49
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.800
If someone in your life
drops off at the end of sentences, 

50
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.432
then emphasize the ends of your own sentences.

51
00:03:08.432 --> 00:03:11.100
Finish strong.

52
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.227
The chameleon effect does not work on everyone.

53
00:03:18.227 --> 00:03:26.100
I myself am very susceptible to it. I find myself mimicking others unintentionally, a lot. 

54
00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.500
I have linked an article from my website
about the chameleon effect 

55
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:35.040
in the frame below the video.
I find it fascinating.

56
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.320
Okay let's get started on our 
lip reading practice for today.

57
00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.060
As always, the answers are in the 
section below the video frame. 

58
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:53.519
Allow yourself some time to get warmed up.
If you're having difficulty after the first  

59
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.440
few sentences,
Stop. 

60
00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.620
Go to the section below the video frame
and follow the instructions on what to do 

61
00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:05.700
to make it easier.
Then make sure you come back! 

62
